# tidydown

## What This Is

A CLI tool that automatically organizes files in your Downloads folder by category. Watches for new files and moves them into appropriate subfolders (Documents, Images, Videos, etc.) based on file extensions. Runs as a background service on macOS and Linux.

## Core Value

Users should never have to manually organize their Downloads folder again.

## Requirements

### Validated

- ✓ File watching - Monitors watch folder for new files and organizes them automatically
- ✓ Categorization - Maps file extensions to category folders (configurable)
- ✓ Conflict resolution - Handles duplicate filenames with configurable strategies (number, timestamp, skip)
- ✓ CLI commands - start, install, uninstall, status, config
- ✓ Background service - Runs as launchd (macOS) or systemd (Linux) service
- ✓ Configuration - YAML/JSON config with categories, watch path, exclude patterns

### Active

- [ ] Manual sort command - Trigger one-time re-organization of the entire watch folder via `tidydown sort`
- [ ] Dry-run mode - Preview what would change without moving files
- [ ] TUI progress - Visual feedback during manual sort with progress animation
- [ ] Skip organized - Only process loose files, ignore files already in category folders

### Out of Scope

- Windows support - macOS and Linux only (platform-specific service integration)
- Automatic categorization of unknown file types - Files without matching extensions stay in root
- Network file systems - Local filesystems only
- Undo functionality - Once files are moved, they stay moved

## Context

**Existing codebase:**
- TypeScript 5.9.3, Node.js 20
- Event-driven architecture with CLI/Core/Config/Service/Utils layers
- yargs for CLI, Zod for config validation, chokidar for file watching
- Result<T,E> pattern for error handling throughout

**User workflow:**
1. User installs tidydown via npm
2. Runs `tidydown install` to set up background service
3. Service watches ~/Downloads (or configured path) and auto-organizes new files
4. User periodically wants to run manual cleanup on accumulated files

## Constraints

- **Platform**: macOS and Linux only - uses platform-specific service managers
- **Runtime**: Node.js 20+ - required for ESNext modules and built-in APIs
- **Type Safety**: TypeScript strict mode - all new code must be typed
- **Backward Compatibility**: Cannot break existing config files or service behavior

